<section xml:id="cmse381-001-lec14-morekfoldcv-worksheet" component="ws14">
  <title>Day 14 Worksheet</title>
  <introduction>
    <program language="python">
      # Everyone's favorite standard imports
      import numpy as np
      import pandas as pd
      import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
      import seaborn as sns
      %matplotlib inline

      from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression,LogisticRegression
      from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error
    </program>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="cmse381-001-lec14-morekfoldcv-worksheet-ptx-1-setting-k-fold-up-on-a-slightly-more-complicated-data-set">
    <title>1. Setting <m>k</m>-fold up on a slightly more complicated data set.</title>
    <p>Ok, let's see how we can use <m>k</m>-fold CV for determining hyperparameters. Below, we're going to generate a data set that is clearly non-linear.</p>
    <program language="python">
      # Set the seed so everyone has the same numbers
      np.random.seed(42)

      def f(t, m1 = -7,m2 = 5, m3 = -.8, b = 6):
          return m3 * t**3 + m2*t**2 + m1*t+b

      n = 300
      X_toy = np.random.uniform(-1,5,n)
      y_toy = f(X_toy) + np.random.normal(0,2,n)

      plt.scatter(X_toy,y_toy)


      # Doing this so the plot isn't ugly
      X_plot = X_toy.copy()
      X_plot.sort()
      plt.plot(X_plot,f(X_plot),c = 'red')


      X_toy = X_toy.reshape(-1,1)
      y_toy = y_toy.reshape(-1,1)
    </program>
    <p>To do this, we are going to set up a polynomial model. For a fixed degree <m>p</m>, we want to use the model
    <md>y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X+ \beta_2 X^2+ \cdots+ \beta_p X^p</md></p>
    <p>Before messing with this on our big data set, let's see how we can trick linear regression into doing our work for us.
    Take a look at my silly input data below.</p>
    <program language="python">
      from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures
    </program>
    <program language="python">
      X = np.array([-3,-2,-1,0, 1,2,3,4,5]).reshape(-1,1)

      p = 4
      poly = PolynomialFeatures(p)
      X_powers = poly.fit_transform(X)

      X_powers
    </program>
    <program language="python">
      p = 4
      poly = PolynomialFeatures(p, include_bias=False)
      X_powers = poly.fit_transform(X)

      X_powers
    </program>
    <p>The trick in all this is that if I pass in this matrix to linear regression, the resulting model learned is exactly the model
    <md>y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X+ \beta_2 X^2+ \cdots+ \beta_p X^p</md>
    we wanted to use earlier so long as I line up the coefficients properly.</p>
    <!-- horizontal rule in source notebook -->
    <exercises>
      <title>Exercises</title>
      <exercisegroup>
        <title>Practice</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>Complete the following exercises.</p>
        </introduction>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Given this input data, what is each column in the following matrix?</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Compare each output column with the original scalar <m>X</m> for a few easy rows such as <m>X=0</m>, <m>1</m>, and <m>2</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><c>PolynomialFeatures(4)</c> produces powers from degree 0 through degree 4.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> The first column of ones represents the degree-zero/bias term; it is not a duplicate observation index.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>The columns are <m>1</m>, <m>X</m>, <m>X^2</m>, <m>X^3</m>, and <m>X^4</m>. The leading column of ones is the degree-zero term used for an intercept/bias.</p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>What did I change from the above code to get the matrix below? What is different about the output matrix?</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Compare the arguments supplied to the two <c>PolynomialFeatures</c> constructors.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Check the number and meaning of columns before and after changing <c>include_bias</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Removing the bias column does not remove the regression intercept when <c>LinearRegression</c> keeps its default <c>fit_intercept=True</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>The second version sets <c>include_bias=False</c>. It removes the leading column of ones, so the matrix contains only <m>X</m>, <m>X^2</m>, <m>X^3</m>, and <m>X^4</m>.</p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>For the original <m>X_{toy}</m> data set, use the <c>LinearRegression</c> class on all of the data (so no train/test splits yet) to train a polynomial model with <m>p=3</m> to predict <m>y</m>. What is the equation of the model learned, including all the values for the coefficients?</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  poly = PolynomialFeatures(3, include_bias=False)
                  X_degree3 = poly.fit_transform(X_toy)
                  model = LinearRegression().fit(..., ...)
                  print(model.intercept_)
                  print(model.coef_)
                </code>
              </program>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Transform the single input into columns <m>X</m>, <m>X^2</m>, and <m>X^3</m> before fitting.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Match <c>coef_[0]</c>, <c>coef_[1]</c>, and <c>coef_[2]</c> to those powers in increasing degree order.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Avoid including both a bias column and a separately fitted intercept unless you intentionally handle the redundancy.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  p = 3
                  poly = PolynomialFeatures(p, include_bias = False)
                  X_powers = poly.fit_transform(X_toy)

                  # X_powers

                  model = LinearRegression()
                  model.fit(X_powers,y_toy)
                  print(f"Coefs: {model.coef_}")
                  print(f"Intercept: {model.intercept_}")

                  # Printing nicely
                  linestring = str(round(model.intercept_[0],2))
                  for i in range(p):
                      linestring += ' + ' + str(round(model.coef_[0,i],2))+ ' X^'+ str(i+1)
                  print( 'y = ', linestring)
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Copy your code from above and modify it to use <m>k</m>-fold cross validation for <m>k=5</m> to approximate the test error with a degree <m>p=3</m> model.</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  poly = PolynomialFeatures(3, include_bias=False)
                  X_degree3 = poly.fit_transform(X_toy)
                  model = LinearRegression()
                  scores = cross_val_score(
                      model, X_degree3, y_toy,
                      scoring='neg_mean_squared_error', cv=...
                  )
                  cv_mse = ...
                </code>
              </program>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>You have easy-mode code from last class that involved the <c>cross_val_score</c> command that would be super useful here.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Pass the degree-three feature matrix, not raw <c>X_toy</c>, to the cross-validation function.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Scikit-learn returns negative MSE scores so that larger scores are better; negate the mean to report MSE.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Taking the mean of negative MSE values without changing the sign produces a negative number, but an MSE cannot be negative.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score
                  from sklearn.model_selection import KFold



                  p = 3
                  poly = PolynomialFeatures(p, include_bias=False)
                  X_powers = poly.fit_transform(X_toy)

                  #build linear regression model
                  model = LinearRegression()

                  #use 5-fold CV to evaluate model
                  scores = cross_val_score(model, X_powers, y_toy, 
                                           scoring='neg_mean_squared_error',
                                           cv=5)

                  #view mean absolute error
                  print(np.average(np.absolute(scores)))


                  #----- Longer version -----#
                  kf = KFold(n_splits=5, shuffle = True)


                  MSEs = []
                  for train_index, test_index in kf.split(X_powers):
                      X_train, X_test = X_powers[train_index], X_powers[test_index]
                      y_train, y_test = y_toy[train_index], y_toy[test_index]


                      model = LinearRegression()
                      model.fit(X_train,y_train)
                      y_hat = model.predict(X_test)

                      MSEs.append(mean_squared_error(y_hat,y_test))
                  print(MSEs)
                  print(np.average(MSEs))
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Using <m>k</m>-fold cross validation for <m>k=5</m>, set up code to approximate the test error for each of the polynomial models below.</p>
              <p><ul>
                <li>
                  <p><m>y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X</m></p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p><m>y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X + \beta_2 X^2</m></p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p><m>y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X+ \beta_2 X^2+ \beta_3 X^3</m></p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p><m>y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X+ \beta_2 X^2+ \beta_3 X^3+ \beta_4 X^4</m></p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p><m>y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X+ \beta_2 X^2+ \beta_3 X^3+ \beta_4 X^4+ \beta_5 X^5</m></p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p><m>y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X+ \beta_2 X^2+ \beta_3 X^3+ \beta_4 X^4+ \beta_5 X^5+ \beta_6 X^6</m></p>
                </li>
              </ul></p>
              <p>Then plot your resulting test errors for each degree. What is the best choice of polynomial for this data set?</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  degrees = range(1, 7)
                  cv_errors = []
                  for degree in degrees:
                      X_degree = PolynomialFeatures(degree, include_bias=False).fit_transform(X_toy)
                      scores = cross_val_score(..., scoring='neg_mean_squared_error', cv=...)
                      cv_errors.append(...)

                  plt.plot(degrees, cv_errors, marker='o')
                </code>
              </program>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Build a new polynomial feature matrix for each degree, then evaluate the same regression estimator with the same five-fold scheme.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Plot degree against average validation MSE and select the degree with the smallest validation error.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Use the same folds when comparing degrees if possible; changing both the model degree and random partition adds unnecessary noise to the comparison.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures
                  from sklearn.model_selection import KFold


                  topDegree = 12 +1
                  poly = PolynomialFeatures(topDegree)
                  X_powers = poly.fit_transform(X_toy.reshape(-1,1))
                  # print(X_powers[:10,:])

                  kf = KFold(n_splits=5, shuffle = True)

                  AllTestErrors = []
                  AllTrainErrors = []
                  for d in range(1,topDegree):
                  #     print(d)
                      X = X_powers[:,1:d+1]
                  #     print(X[:3,:])
                      TestMSEs = []
                      TrainMSEs = []
                      for train_index, test_index in kf.split(X):
                          X_train, X_test = X[train_index], X[test_index]
                          y_train, y_test = y_toy[train_index], y_toy[test_index]


                          model = LinearRegression()
                          model.fit(X_train,y_train)

                          #Get test errors
                          y_hat = model.predict(X_test)
                          TestMSEs.append(mean_squared_error(y_hat,y_test))

                          # Get training errors
                          y_hat_train = model.predict(X_train)
                          TrainMSEs.append(mean_squared_error(y_hat_train,y_train))

                      AllTestErrors.append(np.average(TestMSEs))
                      AllTrainErrors.append(np.average(TrainMSEs))

                  plt.plot(range(1,topDegree), AllTestErrors, label = 'Test')
                  plt.plot(range(1,topDegree), AllTrainErrors, label = 'Train')
                  plt.legend()
                  plt.axis('square')
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>If you still have some time, try the following:</p>
